# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GraphLaw N3 judgment rules — Autonomic Standing Factory case study.
#
# Verdict facts (praxis:ProductionReadyForDeclaredScope /
# praxis:PilotReadyWithExternalSideEffects / praxis:NotReadyWithReasons)
# are derived as `rdf:type` class memberships on the case-study subject
# (`?cs a praxis:<Verdict>`), never via a shared literal-valued predicate
# like praxis:hasVerdict — this crate's Datalog stratifier
# (crates/praxis-graphlaw/src/datalog.rs::relation_of) groups facts by
# PREDICATE for ordinary triples but by OBJECT CLASS for `a`/rdf:type
# triples (the standard Datalog-over-RDF heuristic its own doc comment
# names). Three verdict facts sharing one predicate with three different
# object constants would collapse into a single "relation" for
# stratification purposes, and this file's mutually-exclusive verdict
# rules negate each other's conclusions — that would create a same-
# relation negative self-loop and be rejected as unstratifiable. Typing
# each verdict as its own class keeps every verdict fact its own
# stratification relation, which is what makes the negation below safe.
#
# The three ...Conform/...NoDenialsFound gate facts this file's rules
# consume (praxis:ShaclShapesConform, praxis:ShexSchemaConforms,
# praxis:NoDenialsFound) are injected as plain asserted triples by
# src/bin/case_study_judge.rs AFTER it has actually run validate_shacl /
# validate_shex_c / check_denials against the real merged graph, then
# materialize() is called a second time so these rules can fire off them
# — see that file's module doc for the exact two-pass order. No fact in
# this file's head is ever hand-picked; every verdict is a conjunction
# read back via SPARQL after materialization reaches fixpoint.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@prefix praxis: <https://praxis.dev/ontology/standing#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# --- EvidenceComplete: the case study points at a standing envelope that
#     itself carries a sourced timestamp and >=1 evidence reference. ---
{
  ?cs praxis:hasEvidence ?env .
  ?env praxis:generatedAtUtc ?t .
  ?env praxis:hasEvidence ?ref .
} => { ?cs a praxis:EvidenceComplete } .

# --- StandingInputsValid: evidence is complete AND every externally-
#     computed gate (SHACL/ShEx/denials — injected by the judge bin)
#     passed. ---
{
  ?cs a praxis:EvidenceComplete .
  ?cs a praxis:ShaclShapesConform .
  ?cs a praxis:ShexSchemaConforms .
  ?cs a praxis:NoDenialsFound .
} => { ?cs a praxis:StandingInputsValid } .

# --- ProcessEvidenceValid: a REAL (not not-yet-produced) OCEL log AND a
#     REAL process-validation result are both recorded. ---
{
  ?cs praxis:recordsExecution ?ocel .
  ?cs praxis:validatesProcess ?val .
  not { ?ocel a praxis:NotYetProduced }
  not { ?val a praxis:NotYetProduced }
} => { ?cs a praxis:ProcessEvidenceValid } .

# --- ClientDisplayValid: displayed via a REAL (not not-yet-produced)
#     client route. ---
{
  ?cs praxis:displaysStanding ?route .
  not { ?route a praxis:NotYetProduced }
} => { ?cs a praxis:ClientDisplayValid } .

# --- ExternalSideEffectsSeparated: universally-quantified "no side effect
#     this case study records is a hard blocker", expressed the only way
#     this engine's single-TP `not { ... }` grammar allows a
#     for-all-none-match check: derive an intermediate existential
#     (HasBlockingSideEffect, fired if ANY side effect is blocking), then
#     negate THAT derived fact (shared var ?cs, safe per the range-
#     restriction check) rather than negating an unbound blocking side
#     effect directly. ---
{
  ?cs praxis:hasExternalSideEffect ?e .
  ?e a praxis:BlockingSideEffect .
} => { ?cs a praxis:HasBlockingSideEffect } .

{
  ?cs a praxis:CaseStudy .
  not { ?cs a praxis:HasBlockingSideEffect }
} => { ?cs a praxis:ExternalSideEffectsSeparated } .

# NoTerminalBlockers is derived in readiness.dl.n3 (same engine, loaded
# into the same rule set — see that file's header) from the
# requires/satisfied/critical closure; reused here by reference only.

# --- Verdict: full production readiness for the declared scope. ---
{
  ?cs a praxis:CaseStudy .
  ?cs a praxis:StandingInputsValid .
  ?cs a praxis:ProcessEvidenceValid .
  ?cs a praxis:ClientDisplayValid .
  ?cs a praxis:NoTerminalBlockers .
  ?cs a praxis:ExternalSideEffectsSeparated .
} => { ?cs a praxis:ProductionReadyForDeclaredScope } .

# --- Verdict: pilot-ready — standing inputs valid and no terminal
#     blockers, but not yet fully production-ready (e.g. process/client
#     evidence lanes have not landed), with any side effects separated
#     out as non-blocking rather than counted against readiness. ---
{
  ?cs a praxis:CaseStudy .
  ?cs a praxis:StandingInputsValid .
  ?cs a praxis:NoTerminalBlockers .
  ?cs a praxis:ExternalSideEffectsSeparated .
  not { ?cs a praxis:ProductionReadyForDeclaredScope }
} => { ?cs a praxis:PilotReadyWithExternalSideEffects } .

# --- Verdict: not ready, with receipted reasons (the honest default
#     while Lanes 3-7 have not yet run, or if any hard gate fails). ---
{
  ?cs a praxis:CaseStudy .
  not { ?cs a praxis:ProductionReadyForDeclaredScope }
  not { ?cs a praxis:PilotReadyWithExternalSideEffects }
} => { ?cs a praxis:NotReadyWithReasons } .

# --- Denial: a claim promoted to standing without >=1 evidence relation
#     is a hard integrity violation — never silently accepted. Real
#     "not {}" N3 negation syntax (see rule.rs's DenyHead / `=> false.`
#     head and crates/praxis-graphlaw/tests/datalog_negation.rs for the
#     same negated-body idiom this project already tests). ---
{
  ?claim a praxis:PromotedClaim .
  not { ?claim praxis:hasEvidence ?any }
} => false .
